In a discussion regarding the representation of sea-level rise (SLR) in James's model, specific insights from Yumashev et al. (2021) were explored. The conversation detailed the governing equation for SLR, which incorporates a Gamma distribution for the time constant \( \tau_{\text{SLR}} \). The model utilized recent global SLR percentiles from Nauels et al. (2017) under the SSP5 scenario to calibrate the parameters of this distribution. Optimal values were determined through @Risk Optimiser, resulting in a mean of 386 years and a mode of 362 years, leading to a Monte Carlo mean SLR projection of 1.57 meters by 2100 under RCP8.5, indicating a significant increase over IPCC AR5 estimates. The calibration process is summarized in Supplementary Table 11. James intended to incorporate these elements into the Supplementary Information of a follow-up paper.
